{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 17, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a prominent medieval noble who served as the Count of Tripoli from the year 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born into the powerful House of Tripoli and became a central figure in the politics of the Crusader states during the twelfth century. His early life involved significant challenges as he assumed control of his county while still a minor which required a regency to manage affairs until he reached adulthood. The region of Tripoli faced constant threats from Muslim forces and internal disputes among the Frankish nobility. Raymond eventually secured his position and became one of the most influential leaders in the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond inherited the county after the death of his father Pons who was murdered by assassins linked to the Hashashin sect. This tragic event left young Raymond vulnerable to enemies who sought to seize his lands or undermine his authority. A period of regency followed where experienced nobles guided the administration of the county until Raymond came of age. His successful consolidation of power demonstrated his political acumen and ability to navigate the complex feudal hierarchy of the Levant. He established strong alliances with other Crusader lords and maintained a delicate balance between the interests of the local barons and the needs of the kingdom.\n\n*Role in the Kingdom of Jerusalem*\nThe count played a pivotal role in the succession crisis that erupted after the death of King Baldwin IV. He supported the claim of his cousin Sibylla and her husband Guy of Lusignan despite their controversial reputations. This decision placed him at odds with other powerful factions within the kingdom who opposed Guy as a ruler. Raymond served as regent for the young King Baldwin V and attempted to stabilize the realm during a time of increasing military pressure from Saladin. His diplomatic efforts and military leadership were crucial in maintaining the fragile unity of the Crusader states against external threats.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Death*\nRaymond participated in several major battles that defined the history of the Crusades including the disastrous Battle of Hattin. He had warned against engaging the Muslim army in open combat but was overruled by King Guy. The defeat at Hattin led to the collapse of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the loss of Jerusalem itself. Raymond survived the battle and retreated to Tripoli where he continued to defend his lands against Saladin. He died shortly after these events leaving behind a legacy as a skilled administrator and a loyal but often controversial political operator. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli which would face continued struggles in the following decades. The historical record portrays him as a man of great ability who operated within a context of extreme instability and constant warfare.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0022097188}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0039499225}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.09178419}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0074402224}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -0.99095327}, {"token": " medieval", "logprob": -5.751942}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.026358502}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.60857373}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.63907397}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.00961615}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.0012668644}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.6689314e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.54007196}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.5308712}, {"token": " year", "logprob": -0.06909997}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0019587376}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": "5", "logpr